course: dictionary
lesson: 3284
title: Especially: the word that picks out what matters most
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: The word especially tells you that one thing stands out from the rest. It means more than usual, or more than others. «I love fruit, especially strawberries.» The writer likes all fruit, but strawberries are the one that stands out most.
explain: Especially can also mean for one special purpose or person. «Starting a club can help everyone find friends, especially quiet children who are rarely asked.» Everyone can gain from a club, but especially points to the children who need it most. Ask yourself: which thing is being picked out?
explain: Try a check. Take especially out of the sentence: «I love fruit, strawberries.» The sentence still makes sense, but the reader no longer knows which fruit is the favourite. Especially adds emphasis, so it changes the feeling of a sentence even when the sentence still works without it.
fact: Especially is an adverb, so it tells you more about a verb, an adjective or another adverb.
fact: «Especially» means more than usual, or more than others; particularly.
fact: «Especially» can also mean for one special purpose or person, as in «made especially for you».
fact: «Wildfires and bushfires move very fast, especially with wind.» Here especially shows when they are at their fastest and most dangerous.
fact: Writers often use especially in safety advice, to show when something matters most.
exercise: «I love fruit, especially strawberries.» What does especially show? | Fruit is sour and strange | *Strawberries are the favourite example | The writer is hungry now | Strawberries are very cheap
exercise: «Wildfires move very fast, especially with wind.» What does especially mean here? | Never | Only on Sundays | *Particularly, more than usual | Slowly and quietly
exercise: «Starting a club can help everyone find friends, especially quiet children who are rarely asked.» Which group does the club help most? | Everyone in the school | *Quiet children who are rarely asked | The parents | The teachers
test: «This jacket was made especially for you.» What does especially mean here? | *For one special person | Only on Tuesdays | Too big | Very cheaply
test: Mia says especially is just another word for also. Is she right? | *No, especially picks out one thing that matters most | Yes, they mean exactly the same | Yes, but only in maths | No, especially means quietly
exam: «Moose can be dangerous, especially mothers with calves.» Why does the writer use especially? | To show the moose are sleeping | *To show which moose are most dangerous | To show the moose are small | To count how many moose there are
